.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt IPV6 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ipv6
.Nd the IPv6 protocol: addresses, routes and neighbours
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In netinet/in.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_INET6" "SOCK_STREAM" "0"
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_INET6" "SOCK_DGRAM" "0"
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_INET6" "SOCK_RAW" "IPPROTO_ICMPV6"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel speaks IPv6 beside IPv4 on every card and on
.Ql lo ,
as RFC 8200 has it, with TCP, UDP and ICMPv6
.Pq Xr icmp6 7
over it.
A socket of
.Dv AF_INET6
names its peers by a
.Vt struct sockaddr_in6
of 28 bytes: the family, the port, the flow information, which is not
read, the address in network order, and the scope id, an interface's
index.
A caller may give the 24 bytes of RFC 2133, without the scope id.
A link-local address, fe80::/10, needs a scope id, the index the socket
is bound to by
.Dv SO_BINDTODEVICE ,
or for a UDP send the interface of an
.Dv IPV6_PKTINFO
control message
.Pq Xr send 2 ;
with none it is
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
A socket of
.Dv AF_INET6
reaches IPv4 peers too, by their mapped addresses, ::ffff:A.B.C.D, unless
.Dv IPV6_V6ONLY ,
set before it is bound, keeps it to IPv6
.Pq Xr setsockopt 2 .
A listener bound to :: accepts both, and every address a call returns is
a
.Vt struct sockaddr_in6 .
A connect to a destination no route reaches fails at once with
.Er ENETUNREACH ,
so that a program can try the next address it was given.
.Ss Addresses
An interface holds at most eight IPv6 addresses, each with its prefix
length, scope, flags and lifetimes:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ql lo
holds ::1/128.
.It
A card brought up takes fe80::/64 with the modified EUI-64 of its station
address, joins ff02::1 and starts neighbour discovery.
.It
A card takes from each router advertisement the prefixes it advertises:
an on-link route of each and, for an autonomous /64 while
.Va autoconf
is on, an address of RFC 7217, whose identifier is made from the prefix,
the station address and
.Va stable_secret ,
the same for each boot while the secret is, or the modified EUI-64 while
no secret is written.
With
.Va use_tempaddr
above 0 a temporary address of RFC 8981, with a random identifier, is made
beside it.
The advertisement's RDNSS and DNSSL options, the name servers and search
domains of RFC 8106, go to
.Xr netlink 7 Ns 's
.Dv RTNLGRP_ND_USEROPT
for a program to set the resolver from.
A card whose link goes down forgets these addresses, the routers and their
routes and settings, and its neighbours, as a card taken down does, since
the link may come back on another network; when it comes back the card
detects its addresses again and solicits routers.
.It
A program adds an address with
.Ql ifconfig eth0 add ADDR/LEN
or
.Ql ip -6 addr add ADDR/LEN dev eth0 ,
which goes by
.Xr ioctl 2
or
.Xr netlink 7 ,
and an
.Ic inet6 static
stanza of
.Pa /etc/network/interfaces
does the same at boot.
.El
.Pp
A card's new address is tentative, neither accepted nor used as a source,
for about a second, until duplicate address detection finds no other node
with it; a duplicate is marked
.Dv IFA_F_DADFAILED
and never used.
An address whose preferred lifetime ends is deprecated, used only by the
connections it has, and one whose valid lifetime ends goes, ending those
connections.
A source is chosen for each destination by RFC 6724's rules: an address
of the destination's scope, not deprecated, of the interface the datagram
leaves by, and with the longest prefix in common, a temporary address
first only when
.Va use_tempaddr
is 2.
.Pp
.Ql ifconfig ,
.Ql ip -6 addr
and
.Pa /proc/net/if_inet6
show them.
.Ss Routes
A datagram to ::1 or to one of the machine's own addresses goes by
.Ql lo .
A link-local destination or a group of link scope leaves by the interface
its scope id names.
Another destination takes the route with the longest prefix, then the
lowest metric, from these:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the prefix of each of the cards' addresses, on the link, at metric 256;
.It
the prefixes routers advertise, on the link, at 256, for as long as the
advertisement says;
.It
each router that advertises itself, as a default route at the card's
.Va ra_defrtr_metric ,
1024 unless written, for its router lifetime, a router known to be
unreachable losing to another;
.It
and the routes a program adds, with
.Ql route -A inet6 add
or
.Ql ip -6 route add ,
at 1024 unless a metric is given, at most 32 of these and the routers'
together.
.El
.Pp
.Ql route -A inet6 ,
.Ql ip -6 route
and
.Pa /proc/net/ipv6_route
show them, and
.Ql ip -6 route get ADDR
shows the interface, router and source a datagram to
.Ar ADDR
would take.
.Ss Neighbours
The station address of a next hop on the link is found by neighbour
discovery, RFC 4861, and kept in a cache of 32 entries, each in its
state: incomplete while it is asked for, reachable for about 30 seconds
after an answer, stale after that until it is used, then probed.
A neighbour three solicitations do not answer fails, and each datagram
waiting for it is answered with an ICMPv6 address unreachable from the
machine itself, which the raw ICMPv6 sockets receive.
.Ql ip -6 neigh
shows the cache, and
.Ql ip -6 neigh add ADDR lladdr MAC dev eth0 nud permanent
adds an entry that nothing changes.
.Ss Settings
Each interface has the settings of
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/ Ns Ar NAME ,
which
.Xr proc 5
describes:
.Va accept_ra ,
.Va autoconf ,
.Va disable_ipv6 ,
.Va forwarding ,
.Va ra_defrtr_metric ,
.Va stable_secret
and
.Va use_tempaddr ,
with
.Ql default
for the interfaces to come and
.Ql all
for every one.
.Xr init 8
writes
.Ql default Ns 's
secret at boot from
.Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret ,
made from random bytes at the first boot, so that the machine's addresses
on a network stay the same.
.Ss Datagrams
A datagram longer than its destination's path MTU leaves in fragments,
the path MTU being the link's: the interface's MTU, 1500 bytes unless
set lower, less when a router advertises less or a Packet Too Big lowers
it for that destination, and never below 1280 for a report (RFC 8201); for ::1 and this machine's own addresses it is
.Li lo Ns 's
65536.
Fragments are reassembled, at most 16 datagrams at once, each within 60
seconds of its first fragment.
Hop-by-hop options, routing headers with no segment left and the
fragment header are read; another extension header, AH and ESP among
them, is answered with a parameter problem.
The hop limit is 64, or the routers' advertised one, and 1 to a group,
unless the socket's
.Dv IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS
or
.Dv IPV6_MULTICAST_HOPS
sets another
.Pq Xr setsockopt 2 .
The machine forwards nothing.
.Ss Multicast
A card joins ff02::1 and the solicited-node group of each of its
addresses, with ff02::2 while
.Va forwarding
is on, and reports them to the link's routers by MLD, RFC 3810, or MLDv1
where a querier speaks it.
A datagram or raw socket joins a group with
.Dv IPV6_JOIN_GROUP ,
which the card then reports too.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret -compact
.It Pa /proc/net/if_inet6
The addresses.
.It Pa /proc/net/ipv6_route
The routes.
.It Pa /proc/net/snmp6
The counters of IPv6, ICMPv6 and UDP over IPv6.
.It Pa /proc/net/tcp6 , /proc/net/udp6 , /proc/net/raw6
The IPv6 sockets, which
.Ic netstat
reads;
.Xr ss 8
asks
.Dv NETLINK_SOCK_DIAG
for them instead.
.It Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf
The settings.
.It Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret
The stable secret kept across boots.
.It Pa /usr/share/udhcpc/default6.script
The script of BusyBox's DHCPv6 client,
.Ic udhcpc6 ,
for a network whose routers send their hosts to a server.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On QEMU's user network a guest has its link-local address and one from
the prefix fec0::/64 that QEMU's router advertises:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ip -6 addr
# route -A inet6
# cat /proc/net/if_inet6
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ping 1 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr icmp6 7 ,
.Xr netlink 7 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr netup 8
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 8200, IPv6;
RFC 4861, neighbour discovery;
RFC 4862, address autoconfiguration;
RFC 7217, stable addresses;
RFC 8981, temporary addresses;
RFC 6724, address selection;
RFC 8201, path MTU discovery;
RFC 3810, MLDv2.
